  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 5–6, the military situation in the Strait of Hormuz and surrounding Gulf sharply deteriorated. CENTCOM confirmed it shot down four Iranian one-way attack drones launched toward the strait and then struck Iranian coastal surveillance radar sites at Goruk and on Qeshm Island. The IRGC subsequently reported it had fired on four tankers attempting to transit without its authorisation and launched seven ballistic missiles at US bases in Kuwait and Bahrain. Kuwait's military confirmed seven ballistic missiles entered its airspace, causing material damage but no casualties. US forces defeated the missile and drone attacks, per Stars and Stripes (June 6). Fortune (June 6) reported US naval overwatch had provided escorts to approximately 1,000 commercial vessels over the prior two months — roughly 17 ships per day — a figure that remains far below the pre-crisis ~95/day baseline. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The Iran–US diplomatic channel remained effectively frozen as of the most recent reporting available before June 7. Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi stated on June 3 that "no tangible progress has been made" and confirmed "there is currently no formal process of negotiations between Iran and the United States, but messages are still being exchanged." A senior adviser to Iran's supreme leader characterised negotiations as "at a deadlock" with "the ball in Trump's court" (June 6–7). CNN (June 3) reported that monetary compensation had become a new sticking point, with Trump withholding direct US payment to Iran. A tentative 60-day memorandum of understanding reached on May 28 remained unsigned, blocked by both Trump's pending approval and Iran's Lebanon-linked suspension of talks on June 1. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Israeli domestic politics remained in a holding pattern through the first week of June. The Knesset dissolution bill passed its first reading 106-0 on June 2, but the House Committee did not set an election date, deferring the dispute between Netanyahu's preference for late October and the Haredi parties' preference for early September. No second or third reading had been scheduled as of June 7. Netanyahu concurrently revived the Haredi draft exemption bill in what observers described as an attempt to restore coalition support ahead of elections. The coalition continued to operate at approximately 60 seats, below the 61-seat formal majority threshold, though no formal Knesset roll-call vote confirming this majority loss had been recorded. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, moved <span class="cite">F7</span></p>

      <p>Iranian state media and official government framing of the Hormuz situation escalated in tone through early June. Tasnim News Agency quoted resistance-front statements on June 1 calling for the "complete blocking" of Hormuz and activation of the Bab al-Mandeb corridor as instruments of pressure. PressTV (June 5) framed US-Israeli military presence in the Sea of Oman as "nuclear terrorism" and reported Iranian warships forcing a retreat. IRNA and state television published no new statement from Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei in the June 5–7 window beyond his June 2 declaration that US bases in the region were "no longer safe." The US confirmed he is alive and "increasingly engaging" (Al Jazeera, June 2). → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The humanitarian and commercial shipping picture as of June 7: more than 1,550 commercial vessels remain stranded in and around the strait with approximately 22,500 mariners aboard. DHL projects 4–6 months for shipping normalisation. Congressional testimony placed mine-clearing alone at up to six months. Brent crude markets have priced in continued disruption. Six P&amp;I clubs have withdrawn Persian Gulf cover; Maersk and MSC continue routing via the Cape of Good Hope with no announced return. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F9</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:38%;">Escalation 38%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:42%;">Protracted 42%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:20%;">De-esc 20%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F1 at 35% probability of a new discrete Hormuz closure action before June 9 and the 67% probability of continued diplomatic deadlock (inverse of F4) sustain the escalation pathway through mid-June; the June 6 IRGC missile launches at Kuwait and Bahrain represent the highest operational tempo since the crisis began.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> F5's 85% probability of continued severe Hormuz disruption through July 2 and F9's 82% probability of below-30/day transits through July 15 anchor the protracted scenario as the modal outcome; mine-clearing alone takes 4–6 months by Congressional testimony even if a deal is reached.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> The de-escalation path requires F8 (talks resumed by June 30, p=45%) and a consequent mine-clearing process; given F5 at 15% and F9 at 18% even on 25–38 day horizons, the conditional probability of meaningful de-escalation within forecast horizons is well below F8's probability itself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media's framing as of the June 5–7 window combines two distinct registers: a posture of strategic resistance and a narrative of enemy aggression justifying IRGC actions. IRNA and PressTV foregrounded the IRGC's June 6 drone operations not as offensive escalations but as "warning shots" tied to what they frame as unlawful commercial navigation in waters Iran treats as under its authority during wartime. The phrase "without permission" — referring to tankers attempting to transit without IRGC coordination — is central to how state media justifies the IRGC's actions against four vessels, framing Iranian interdiction of shipping as procedural enforcement, not blockade aggression. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>On the diplomatic track, Iranian state media avoided claiming talks had collapsed permanently. Araghchi's June 3 formulation — "our relationship with the Americans has not been severed, but there has been no progress" — was amplified across IRNA, Tasnim, and Fars as evidence that Iran occupies the principled position: prepared to talk, but not under conditions of ongoing Israeli operations in Lebanon. This framing serves the dual purpose of keeping Western and regional audiences from concluding Iran has abandoned diplomacy, while placing pressure on the US to constrain Israel. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>The new supreme leader Mojtaba Khamenei's June 2 declaration that US military bases in the region were "no longer safe" received prominent placement in state television programming and was described by PressTV as a "strategic deterrence signal." State media published no Khamenei statement in the June 5–7 window — an absence during a military escalation weekend that is itself notable in the state-media framing landscape, given his June 2 declaration was the most prominent IRGC-adjacent statement of the preceding week. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

